{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-214096","model_call":30,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ stoners motivation dopamine marijuana)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ stoners motivation dopamine marijuana)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_470921906#1_941960490","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q638","rank":1,"snippet":"THE BASICS \" , \"segment\": \"I have written extensively about dopamine and endocannabinoids (self-produced cannabis) in The Athlete ’s Way over the years. The new study , released on July 1, 2013, was conducted by scientists at Imperial College London, UCL and King's College London. The researchers found that long-term cannabis users tend to produce less dopamine, a neurochemical directly linked to motivation and reward. Using PET brain imaging, the researchers found that <cover>dopamine levels in a part of the brain called the striatum were lowest in people who smoke more cannabis and those who began smoking marijuana at a younger age. The findings suggest why \"stoners\" are stereotypically viewed as lacking the motivation </cover>to work hard to pursue their dreams or to be ambitious. Do you think that pot makes people lazy? Have you had personal experience with the phenomenon of cannabis-induced “amotivational syndrome”—also known as being a slacker? There is a strong link between dopamine and the CB-1 and CB-2 cannabinoid receptors of the brain. Any exogenous substance, like cannabis, hijacks the pre-existing receptors for the endogenously produced neurochemical. Contrary to popular belief, endocannabinoids are more strongly ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_470921906#1_941960490","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":41},"model_calls":30,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
