{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-214096","model_call":31,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q638"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q638"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_603975812#17_1085244644","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q638","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_603975812#17_1085244644\" , \"end_char\": 8911.000000 , \"headings\": \" \" , \"segment\": \"alphaspirit/Shutterstock) The short answer: maybe. Marijuana causes a temporary release of <cover>dopamine, which theoretically raises motivation in the short-term. However, the effects of marijuana on the brain are complicated and vary depending on the strain. Certain strains of marijuana are known for increasing energy and alertness (sativa strains) while others are more sedative and have a “couch-lock” effect (indica strains). Users generally agree that sativa strains are a better choice for having a productive day. Likewise, many people report that using marijuana actually increases their motivation, based on their own personal experience. There are many well-known successful stoners, </cover>including Carl Sagan, Bob Marley, Jimi Hendrix, and even Bill Gates. If they were locked to their couches in a pot-induced haze, could they really achieve that level of success? A few studies seem to support the idea that marijuana can boost motivation.\" , \"start_char\": 7967.000000 , \"title\":"},"time":14},"model_calls":31,"document_judgments":18,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
