{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-214096","model_call":55,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"marijuana causes the munchies\" thc olfactory dopamine)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"marijuana causes the munchies\" thc olfactory dopamine)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1163123121#3_2310998800","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q653","rank":1,"snippet":"did not demonstrate an increased appetite when given the drug, showing that the \"munchies\" effect was dependent on <cover>olfactory lobe activity as well. The upshot of all this: If mice are an accurate model for humans, one of the ways that THC increases appetite is by making us more sensitive to the smells of food. Because scent and taste are so closely related, it likely allows us to better taste flavors as well. This new finding is likely just a piece of the THC-and-appetite puzzle. Previous research has found that the drug also acts on receptors in a region of the brain called the nucleus accumbens, increasing the release of the neurotransmitter dopamine —and the sensation of pleasure—that comes as a result of eating while high. Other work has found that THC additionally interacts with the same sorts of receptors in the hypothalamus, leading to release of the hormone ghrelin, which stimulates hunger.\" , \"start_char\": 2688.000000 , \"title\": \"A Scientific Explanation of How Marijuana Causes the Munchies  | </cover>Science   | Smithsonian Magazine\" , \"url\": \"https://www.smithsonianmag.com/science-nature/scientific-explanation-","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1163123121#3_2310998800","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":23},"model_calls":55,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
